= BITwiki Charter =

The Charter defines BITwiki's purpose, research character, epistemic commitments, stewardship principles, and operating covenant. It is not the [[BITwiki:Manifesto|Manifesto]], which declares direction, and it is not the [[BITwiki:Constitution|Constitution]], which records durable system invariants.

== Preamble ==

BITwiki is a living knowledge system: machine-queryable, human-legible, source-aware, and continuously revisable.

It is a professional research and knowledge space, not an oracle of final truth. It does not claim perfect neutrality or complete knowledge. It exists to make what is known, believed, inferred, disputed, modeled, and imagined more inspectable—together with the evidence, perspective, uncertainty, and provenance that give those claims their proper context.

The system is curated. Authority, stewardship, and publication boundaries should be explicit rather than disguised as decentralization merely because networked or agentic technology is involved.

== Article I — Purpose ==

=== Mission ===

BITwiki exists to create a '''machine-queryable, human-legible knowledge ecosystem''' that captures structured knowledge with explicit epistemology, enables ontological retrieval beyond text search, preserves multiple perspectives with explicit provenance, and maintains fidelity as knowledge moves between research, discussion, computation, implementation, and durable memory.

=== Vision ===

A mature BITwiki should make knowledge:

* transparent in its uncertainty;
* traceable to its sources;
* accessible at multiple levels of expertise;
* modular and reusable without losing context;
* able to evolve through recursive refinement;
* resistant to noise, filler, and false certainty;
* capable of connecting theory, evidence, history, implementation, and consequence.

=== Scope ===

Systems science, cybernetics, information theory, complexity, biology, philosophy, mathematics, technology, engineering, and related domains remain important roots, but BITwiki is not limited to a single discipline. Its scope is determined by whether material can become durable, meaningful, well-contextualized knowledge rather than by disciplinary borders alone.

Ephemeral news, promotional copy, unsupported assertion presented as fact, private material, and content without durable knowledge value should not be crystallized merely to increase volume.

== Article II — Epistemic commitments ==

=== Radical epistemic humility ===

'''All knowledge is provisional.''' No page, model, agent, editor, source, institution, or constitutional document receives automatic epistemic privilege.

Human observers have biological and cultural filters. Machine systems have training, architecture, tool, context, and inference limits. BITwiki should make those limitations visible where they matter.

V1 introduced the idea of a '''confidence trail''' rather than treating confidence as only a number: a traversable path from observation to conclusion through sources, methods, alternatives, limitations, relationships, and revision history.

A confidence trail is not a score stamped upon a claim. It is a topology of justification that another reader or agent can inspect.

=== Radical skepticism ===

No claim is immune from interrogation, including claims about the system itself.

* Question assumptions.
* Test methods as well as conclusions.
* State what could change an assessment.
* Treat selective skepticism as a possible form of disguised dogma.
* Preserve contradictory or limiting evidence when it materially changes interpretation.

Self-reference includes self-doubt.

=== Radical / operational realism ===

The Charter lineage uses both ''radical realism'' and ''hyper-realism''. Their shared operational concern is stronger than either label: reality is not established by popularity, credentials, or institutional prestige alone; models and claims should be judged through evidence, logical coherence, predictive or explanatory power, survival of refutation, and practical validity where those tests apply.

The historical Charter sometimes stated this aggressively as ''ontology is instrument, not mirror'' and ''fidelity to dynamics over appearance''. V2 preserves that formulation as lineage without requiring every domain to adopt one theory of truth.

=== Nullification of non-signals ===

'''Signal preservation requires noise suppression.'''

BITwiki should reject filler, performative excess, corporate language without informational content, duplicated scaffolding, and complexity that carries no meaning.

The goal is not minimal word count. It is '''compression without loss of meaning'''. Depth, nuance, ambiguity, contradiction, and unusual language are signal when they materially carry the thought.

=== Uncertainty ===

Uncertainty is not a defect to hide. Mark what is observed, inferred, hypothetical, contested, model-dependent, or unknown when those distinctions matter.

Numerical confidence may be useful in some domains, but it is not a universal substitute for evidence structure. The canonical epistemic model belongs in [[BITwiki:Epistemics]].

== Article III — Evidence and methods ==

=== Traceability ===

Important claims should be traceable to their sources. Primary sources are preferred when they are the appropriate evidence, but source pedigree does not replace direct assessment.

A source should be understood in context:

* what it establishes;
* what it does not establish;
* how directly it bears on the claim;
* what limitations or incentives affect it;
* what other evidence supports or challenges it.

=== Context-dependent evidence ===

Different domains validate knowledge differently. Mathematical proof, empirical observation, engineering performance, historical documentation, logical deduction, phenomenological report, adversarial testing, and distributed cognition are not interchangeable forms of evidence.

The method must fit the question.

=== Assessment over pedigree ===

Credentials, peer review, institutional reputation, and consensus are informative signals, not epistemic trump cards. Work should remain inspectable on its own merits.

Peer review is valuable when the review process is competent, relevant, and independent. It should neither be worshipped nor dismissed categorically.

== Article IV — Pluralism and perspective ==

BITwiki does not pretend that perspective disappears when prose becomes technical.

Multiple scientific, engineering, philosophical, historical, religious, ecological, esoteric, artistic, and other lenses may be represented when they contribute meaningful knowledge or interpretation.

The requirements are explicitness and honesty:

* evidence is not metaphor;
* metaphor is not measurement;
* worldview is not established fact;
* minority interpretation is not automatically false;
* consensus is not automatically true;
* speculation is welcome when labeled as speculation.

Pluralism should increase the resolution of the knowledge field, not dissolve standards of evidence.

== Article V — Purposeful recursion ==

BITwiki is recursive because the knowledge system can inspect and improve its own knowledge practices. Recursion is not valuable merely because it is self-referential.

A recursive process should have a target, a scope, and a reason to continue.

Bootstrap when bootstrapping serves a function. Self-correct when correction addresses a real flaw. Revisit a framework when evidence, use, or contradiction gives reason to revisit it.

'''Recursion without purpose is empty motion.''' The loop should tighten understanding, close a gap, expose an assumption, improve a tool, or produce some other genuine refinement.

== Article VI — Human–AI symbiosis and authorship ==

BITwiki emerged from human–AI collaboration. V1 explored several models of that relationship, from agent-mediated authorship to the performative H=1 / mitochondria analogy in the separate [[Charter]].

V2 does not force one metaphysical interpretation of human–AI symbiosis. It preserves the durable operational requirement:

* humans retain agency and responsibility;
* agents and models remain inspectable and correctable;
* machine capacity should extend human capacity rather than manufacture dependency;
* provenance should distinguish human direction, machine generation, external source material, and later editorial transformation when that distinction matters.

The human should emerge with more clarity, dignity, self-respect, and agency—not less.

== Article VII — Stewardship and governance ==

The V1 Charter lineage explicitly rejected the fiction that BITwiki was a decentralized autonomous organization and repeatedly stated that BITCORE maintained editorial coherence.

V2 preserves the underlying governance invariant: '''authority must be explicit'''. A curated knowledge system should not imply distributed sovereignty merely because many agents, tools, contributors, or interfaces participate in production.

Stewardship includes:

* maintaining content quality and provenance;
* protecting public/private boundaries;
* resolving structural conflicts;
* keeping schema and navigation coherent;
* documenting consequential editorial decisions;
* preserving revision history and source lineage;
* ensuring that automation remains subordinate to knowledge quality.

Specific operational roles may evolve without rewriting this Charter every time the runtime changes.

== Article VIII — Technical commitments ==

=== Machine readability ===
Structured data, semantic markup, stable identifiers, relationships, and queryability should exist where they increase retrieval and reuse.

=== Human legibility ===
Clear prose, progressive disclosure, useful navigation, and coherent canonical pages remain first-class requirements. Machine structure must not make the wiki hostile to human reading.

=== Interoperability ===
Knowledge should be exportable and portable through standard formats wherever practical. The system should avoid unnecessary lock-in to one interface, model, or runtime.

=== Sustainability ===
Long-term preservation requires revision history, backups, provenance, reproducible source control, and deliberate migration procedures.

== Article IX — Amendment and revision ==

The Charter is revisionable, including its own assumptions.

Major amendments should:

# preserve the previous text and revision history;
# state what changed;
# state why it changed;
# identify the source or pressure that motivated the change;
# distinguish philosophical revision from implementation maintenance;
# avoid silently rewriting historical branches as though they had always agreed.

